WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.640
Most people think of the holidays as the

00:00:01.920 --> 00:00:05.760
time of year where you spend a lot of

00:00:03.640 --> 00:00:07.640
money, but for some people it's the time

00:00:05.760 --> 00:00:09.400
of year where you can make the most of

00:00:07.640 --> 00:00:12.080
it. And I'm not talking about working

00:00:09.400 --> 00:00:14.080
extra hours or picking up extra shifts.

00:00:12.080 --> 00:00:15.920
I'm talking about simple services people

00:00:14.080 --> 00:00:17.920
are more than happy to pay for because

00:00:15.920 --> 00:00:19.920
it's the busiest time of year and nobody

00:00:17.920 --> 00:00:21.400
has time to do it themselves. And one

00:00:19.920 --> 00:00:25.200
woman that I'm going to show you built a

00:00:21.400 --> 00:00:26.880
business that brought in over $316,000

00:00:25.200 --> 00:00:29.600
in a single year doing something you

00:00:26.880 --> 00:00:31.120
probably done yourself every single

00:00:29.600 --> 00:00:32.960
Christmas. So today I'm going to be

00:00:31.120 --> 00:00:35.120
breaking down the five holiday

00:00:32.960 --> 00:00:37.120
businesses that you can start right now

00:00:35.120 --> 00:00:38.920
going into holiday season. And my name

00:00:37.120 --> 00:00:40.360
is Austin Zaback and I've built multiple

00:00:38.920 --> 00:00:42.280
different businesses over the years

00:00:40.360 --> 00:00:43.920
primarily in real estate, pest control,

00:00:42.280 --> 00:00:45.720
and a handful of other industries over

00:00:43.920 --> 00:00:47.440
the last decade. And if you get any

00:00:45.720 --> 00:00:49.080
value out of today's video, the only

00:00:47.440 --> 00:00:50.920
thing that I ask you to do is that you

00:00:49.080 --> 00:00:53.040
smash that like button for the YouTube

00:00:50.920 --> 00:00:54.880
algorithm, subscribe by the end of the

00:00:53.040 --> 00:00:56.480
video if you get any value, it'll help

00:00:54.880 --> 00:00:58.040
us get this video in front of more

00:00:56.480 --> 00:01:00.000
people who need to see it. So let's

00:00:58.040 --> 00:01:02.000
start with one that has the biggest real

00:01:00.000 --> 00:01:04.040
number in this entire video and that is

00:01:02.000 --> 00:01:06.400
mobile gift wrapping. So Michelle

00:01:04.040 --> 00:01:08.480
Hensley runs Nifty Package Co and she

00:01:06.400 --> 00:01:11.200
brings professional gift wrapping right

00:01:08.480 --> 00:01:13.880
to people's homes, offices, and events.

00:01:11.200 --> 00:01:16.040
And in December of 2024 in an interview

00:01:13.880 --> 00:01:19.760
with Entrepreneur, she said gift

00:01:16.040 --> 00:01:22.680
wrapping brought in over $316,000

00:01:19.760 --> 00:01:24.680
in 2023. And that was about 68% of the

00:01:22.680 --> 00:01:26.640
company's sales. Now that's the whole

00:01:24.680 --> 00:01:30.320
wrapping operation, not one person's

00:01:26.640 --> 00:01:32.200
paycheck, but still over $316,000

00:01:30.320 --> 00:01:34.000
from wrapping presents. Think about it.

00:01:32.200 --> 00:01:35.920
Every December people are sitting on

00:01:34.000 --> 00:01:38.120
their living room floor at midnight

00:01:35.920 --> 00:01:40.320
wrapping gifts, running out of tape, and

00:01:38.120 --> 00:01:42.160
hating every single minute of it. And

00:01:40.320 --> 00:01:43.840
for a business it's even worse. If a

00:01:42.160 --> 00:01:45.920
company is sending out gifts to a

00:01:43.840 --> 00:01:48.280
hundred different clients, somebody has

00:01:45.920 --> 00:01:51.440
to wrap every single one of those. Nifty

00:01:48.280 --> 00:01:54.880
Packages charges $190 an hour for one

00:01:51.440 --> 00:01:57.000
wrapper, 295 an hour for two, and 445 an

00:01:54.880 --> 00:01:59.320
hour for three with a minimum of two

00:01:57.000 --> 00:02:01.400
hours. And paper and ribbon are included

00:01:59.320 --> 00:02:04.760
in that. They also price by the gift.

00:02:01.400 --> 00:02:07.880
So, $25 for a small gift, $35 for a

00:02:04.760 --> 00:02:10.360
shirt box, and $45 for a shoe box size

00:02:07.880 --> 00:02:12.760
gift. So, at one wrapper, a two-hour

00:02:10.360 --> 00:02:14.440
appointment is $380.

00:02:12.760 --> 00:02:17.120
If you do eight appointments, that's a

00:02:14.440 --> 00:02:20.120
little over 3,000. 16 appointments is a

00:02:17.120 --> 00:02:22.480
little over $6,000. And 16 appointments

00:02:20.120 --> 00:02:24.280
over 4 weeks is just four appointments a

00:02:22.480 --> 00:02:26.480
week, which is about eight hours of

00:02:24.280 --> 00:02:28.520
actual wrapping plus your prep and your

00:02:26.480 --> 00:02:30.440
drive time. Now, the startup cost here

00:02:28.520 --> 00:02:32.080
is about as low as it can possibly get

00:02:30.440 --> 00:02:34.120
that we've talked about on this channel.

00:02:32.080 --> 00:02:36.200
You're looking at probably about 500 to

00:02:34.120 --> 00:02:37.640
1,000 dollars to get started. And that's

00:02:36.200 --> 00:02:40.200
going to be your wrapping paper, your

00:02:37.640 --> 00:02:42.760
ribbon, tissue, tags, tape, scissors, a

00:02:40.200 --> 00:02:44.560
folding table, and some bins to carry it

00:02:42.760 --> 00:02:46.320
all in and a little bit of marketing.

00:02:44.560 --> 00:02:48.080
You don't need a branded van. You can

00:02:46.320 --> 00:02:49.959
literally do this out of the car that

00:02:48.080 --> 00:02:51.920
you already have. But, the real money

00:02:49.959 --> 00:02:54.400
here is in corporate. You want to go

00:02:51.920 --> 00:02:56.720
after office managers, real estate

00:02:54.400 --> 00:02:59.480
teams, boutiques, and companies that

00:02:56.720 --> 00:03:01.840
send holiday gifts to their clients. One

00:02:59.480 --> 00:03:04.120
corporate order can mean a whole pile of

00:03:01.840 --> 00:03:07.280
gifts in one location. So, you can set

00:03:04.120 --> 00:03:09.440
up one time and wrap the entire day. You

00:03:07.280 --> 00:03:11.880
can even offer branded packaging for

00:03:09.440 --> 00:03:14.160
them or run a wrapping station at their

00:03:11.880 --> 00:03:15.920
holiday event. And this one comes back

00:03:14.160 --> 00:03:17.680
every single year, which is what I love

00:03:15.920 --> 00:03:19.400
about it. The households that book you

00:03:17.680 --> 00:03:21.640
this year will book you again. And the

00:03:19.400 --> 00:03:23.519
companies that send gifts every December

00:03:21.640 --> 00:03:25.080
are going to need you, well, every

00:03:23.519 --> 00:03:26.959
December. All right, number two on

00:03:25.080 --> 00:03:29.120
today's list is for anybody who's good

00:03:26.959 --> 00:03:31.800
at making things look nice. And that is

00:03:29.120 --> 00:03:34.239
Christmas tree decorating. Amanda Ware

00:03:31.800 --> 00:03:36.160
runs a company called Hello Holidays in

00:03:34.239 --> 00:03:38.840
Bentonville, Arkansas. And back in

00:03:36.160 --> 00:03:41.640
December of 2022, she told Newsweek she

00:03:38.840 --> 00:03:43.959
was charging anywhere from 300 to 1,500

00:03:41.640 --> 00:03:45.880
dollars a tree with an average of about

00:03:43.959 --> 00:03:47.840
a thousand dollars. She said she was

00:03:45.880 --> 00:03:50.440
decorating at least a hundred trees a

00:03:47.840 --> 00:03:52.720
season, and each one took her two to

00:03:50.440 --> 00:03:54.640
five hours. And on top of that, she was

00:03:52.720 --> 00:03:56.880
selling ornaments, ribbon, and other

00:03:54.640 --> 00:03:58.760
decorations as well. Now, those are her

00:03:56.880 --> 00:04:01.080
numbers from a few years ago, not what

00:03:58.760 --> 00:04:03.440
she's charging today. But, they do show

00:04:01.080 --> 00:04:05.240
you how far this business can actually

00:04:03.440 --> 00:04:07.680
go. Now, here's what's interesting about

00:04:05.240 --> 00:04:09.120
this one. The customer usually already

00:04:07.680 --> 00:04:11.000
have everything that you're going to

00:04:09.120 --> 00:04:12.800
need. They've got the tree, and they've

00:04:11.000 --> 00:04:14.840
got the boxes of ornaments sitting in

00:04:12.800 --> 00:04:16.799
the garage. What they don't have is they

00:04:14.840 --> 00:04:19.320
don't have the time or the eye to make

00:04:16.799 --> 00:04:20.959
it look like a designer did the job. So,

00:04:19.320 --> 00:04:23.080
they're not paying you for stuff,

00:04:20.959 --> 00:04:25.000
they're paying you for your skill and

00:04:23.080 --> 00:04:27.200
for your time. For a current price,

00:04:25.000 --> 00:04:29.400
another company called Armond's Design

00:04:27.200 --> 00:04:32.480
and Home Decor advertises tree

00:04:29.400 --> 00:04:34.080
decorating packages starting at $350

00:04:32.480 --> 00:04:36.040
when the customer supplies their own

00:04:34.080 --> 00:04:38.520
decorations. So, let's just use that

00:04:36.040 --> 00:04:40.960
math. 350 bucks, if you were to do 10

00:04:38.520 --> 00:04:44.800
trees, we're talking about $3,500. 20

00:04:40.960 --> 00:04:47.400
trees, 7 grand. 30 trees, 10,500. And if

00:04:44.800 --> 00:04:49.600
you do 20 trees over a 5-week period of

00:04:47.400 --> 00:04:52.000
time, that's only four trees a week. At

00:04:49.600 --> 00:04:54.120
3 to 5 hours a tree, that's about 12 to

00:04:52.000 --> 00:04:56.440
20 hours. Plus, your prep and drive

00:04:54.120 --> 00:04:58.400
time, so you could absolutely do this on

00:04:56.440 --> 00:05:00.280
the nights and weekends. Now, to get

00:04:58.400 --> 00:05:02.040
started here, you're looking at probably

00:05:00.280 --> 00:05:05.320
somewhere in the neighborhood of $600 to

00:05:02.040 --> 00:05:07.320
about $1,300 in startup cost. And since

00:05:05.320 --> 00:05:08.960
the customer owns the tree and the

00:05:07.320 --> 00:05:10.680
ornaments, you're not buying any

00:05:08.960 --> 00:05:12.840
inventory. Now, the first thing you need

00:05:10.680 --> 00:05:14.640
here is probably a portfolio. So, what I

00:05:12.840 --> 00:05:16.919
would do is decorate a couple of real

00:05:14.640 --> 00:05:18.800
trees, even if it's your own trees,

00:05:16.919 --> 00:05:20.919
maybe a couple of your friends, and get

00:05:18.800 --> 00:05:22.360
great photos and post those on social

00:05:20.919 --> 00:05:23.840
media. Then, go to your local

00:05:22.360 --> 00:05:26.400
neighborhood groups and connect with

00:05:23.840 --> 00:05:27.760
home organizers, interior designers, and

00:05:26.400 --> 00:05:29.919
cleaning service, because their

00:05:27.760 --> 00:05:31.400
customers are the exact kind of people

00:05:29.919 --> 00:05:33.160
that would want to hire you. Now,

00:05:31.400 --> 00:05:35.160
there's a lot of room to grow from one

00:05:33.160 --> 00:05:36.919
booking. You can come back in January

00:05:35.160 --> 00:05:39.120
and take it all down. You can do the

00:05:36.919 --> 00:05:40.919
mantel, the staircase, and a second

00:05:39.120 --> 00:05:42.600
tree. You can even source new

00:05:40.919 --> 00:05:44.200
decorations for them for the next

00:05:42.600 --> 00:05:46.120
season. And the people who love their

00:05:44.200 --> 00:05:48.560
tree this time of year are going to want

00:05:46.120 --> 00:05:50.080
you back next year and every year after

00:05:48.560 --> 00:05:52.120
that. All right, number three is a

00:05:50.080 --> 00:05:54.560
business where one customer can pay you

00:05:52.120 --> 00:05:56.920
twice in the same season, and that is

00:05:54.560 --> 00:05:59.440
Christmas tree delivery, setup, and

00:05:56.920 --> 00:06:02.520
removal. Han's Christmas Farm in Oregon,

00:05:59.440 --> 00:06:04.800
Wisconsin, run by Greg Han, will deliver

00:06:02.520 --> 00:06:06.920
a tree, set the tree up, and then come

00:06:04.800 --> 00:06:08.680
back after the holidays to take the tree

00:06:06.920 --> 00:06:10.720
away. Now, think about everything that

00:06:08.680 --> 00:06:12.520
goes into getting a real tree. You've

00:06:10.720 --> 00:06:14.640
got to tie it to the top of your car,

00:06:12.520 --> 00:06:17.040
drag it into the house, get it standing

00:06:14.640 --> 00:06:19.080
straight stand, and get needles all over

00:06:17.040 --> 00:06:21.400
the floor. And then after Christmas, you

00:06:19.080 --> 00:06:23.720
have to drag that same dried-out tree

00:06:21.400 --> 00:06:25.720
back out and figure out where and how to

00:06:23.720 --> 00:06:27.520
even get rid of it. So, the second part

00:06:25.720 --> 00:06:30.880
is what nobody even thinks about until

00:06:27.520 --> 00:06:33.360
January. Han's charges $130 for delivery

00:06:30.880 --> 00:06:35.960
and setup on a tree up to 8 and 1/2 ft

00:06:33.360 --> 00:06:38.440
and $110 to come back and remove that

00:06:35.960 --> 00:06:40.840
tree. So, you're talking about 240 for

00:06:38.440 --> 00:06:43.480
both visits. Now, bigger trees do cost a

00:06:40.840 --> 00:06:47.160
little bit more. A 9 to 11 and 1/2 ft

00:06:43.480 --> 00:06:50.280
tree is $330 for both, and a 12 to 13

00:06:47.160 --> 00:06:52.360
and 1/2 ft tree is $380 for both. And if

00:06:50.280 --> 00:06:55.400
somebody just wants it dropped off, then

00:06:52.360 --> 00:06:57.640
delivery alone starts at $95. And the

00:06:55.400 --> 00:06:59.960
setup includes a fresh cut, putting it

00:06:57.640 --> 00:07:02.240
in the stand, watering it. Removal

00:06:59.960 --> 00:07:04.480
includes draining the stand and cleaning

00:07:02.240 --> 00:07:06.160
up the entire mess so the homeowner

00:07:04.480 --> 00:07:08.000
doesn't have to deal with anything. Now,

00:07:06.160 --> 00:07:09.960
the customer does have to have all the

00:07:08.000 --> 00:07:11.640
lights and the ornaments off the tree

00:07:09.960 --> 00:07:13.880
when that person comes back out, so

00:07:11.640 --> 00:07:17.120
you're not stuck doing that part. So, at

00:07:13.880 --> 00:07:19.120
$240 for both visits, 20 customers would

00:07:17.120 --> 00:07:21.360
be about 4,800. 50 customers, we're

00:07:19.120 --> 00:07:23.919
talking about 12 grand. And 100

00:07:21.360 --> 00:07:25.280
customers, we're looking at $24,000.

00:07:23.919 --> 00:07:27.200
Now, what I love about this one is

00:07:25.280 --> 00:07:29.120
again, the startup cost is really low.

00:07:27.200 --> 00:07:31.240
If you already have a pickup truck or

00:07:29.120 --> 00:07:33.520
van or even a trailer, you're probably

00:07:31.240 --> 00:07:35.080
looking at around like 500 to 1,800

00:07:33.520 --> 00:07:36.960
dollars. And that covers moving

00:07:35.080 --> 00:07:38.840
blankets, which are about 10 bucks each

00:07:36.960 --> 00:07:41.440
at Harbor Freight, plus tie-downs,

00:07:38.840 --> 00:07:43.680
tarps, a saw, some gloves, and a wet/dry

00:07:41.440 --> 00:07:45.160
vac so you can vacuum up the mess. One

00:07:43.680 --> 00:07:46.760
thing that I would recommend before you

00:07:45.160 --> 00:07:48.880
start is to check wherever you're

00:07:46.760 --> 00:07:50.920
located to make sure there's a disposal

00:07:48.880 --> 00:07:52.640
site near you that you can take the

00:07:50.920 --> 00:07:53.960
trees to when it's all said and done.

00:07:52.640 --> 00:07:56.360
And the easiest way here to get

00:07:53.960 --> 00:07:58.240
customers is to partner with tree lots

00:07:56.360 --> 00:08:00.360
and nurseries and of course post on

00:07:58.240 --> 00:08:01.800
social media. The customer buys the tree

00:08:00.360 --> 00:08:03.760
from them and then you sell the

00:08:01.800 --> 00:08:06.240
delivery, the setup, and a scheduled

00:08:03.760 --> 00:08:08.040
pickup in January. The lot gets a happy

00:08:06.240 --> 00:08:10.200
customer and you get the job without

00:08:08.040 --> 00:08:12.120
having to sell a single tree. Now, this

00:08:10.200 --> 00:08:14.120
next one can bring in the most money

00:08:12.120 --> 00:08:16.720
from the fewest customers, and that is

00:08:14.120 --> 00:08:18.560
holiday lighting installation. So, Devin

00:08:16.720 --> 00:08:20.320
and Jessie Sutton run the Christmas

00:08:18.560 --> 00:08:21.880
Light Crew in the Tulsa area in

00:08:20.320 --> 00:08:24.040
Oklahoma. And they don't just hang the

00:08:21.880 --> 00:08:25.560
lights, they install them, they maintain

00:08:24.040 --> 00:08:27.240
them throughout the season, and then

00:08:25.560 --> 00:08:29.240
they take them down and they store them

00:08:27.240 --> 00:08:31.200
for you until the next season. Now,

00:08:29.240 --> 00:08:33.159
think about who's hiring these people.

00:08:31.200 --> 00:08:34.919
Hanging Christmas lights means climbing

00:08:33.159 --> 00:08:37.520
a ladder, getting up on a roof in the

00:08:34.919 --> 00:08:39.360
cold, and untangling cords for an entire

00:08:37.520 --> 00:08:41.360
weekend. Then you get to do it all over

00:08:39.360 --> 00:08:43.120
again in January to take them down. And

00:08:41.360 --> 00:08:44.920
a lot of homeowners would much rather

00:08:43.120 --> 00:08:46.560
write a check and have it done right. I

00:08:44.920 --> 00:08:48.640
know for a fact that that includes

00:08:46.560 --> 00:08:50.160
myself. I do not want to deal with all

00:08:48.640 --> 00:08:52.640
that. Now, for pricing here, they

00:08:50.160 --> 00:08:56.160
advertise a basic single-story roofline

00:08:52.640 --> 00:08:57.680
at $499 to $999. A roofline plus extra

00:08:56.160 --> 00:09:00.880
decorations, you're looking at about

00:08:57.680 --> 00:09:03.600
$1,000 to $1,499. And larger displays

00:09:00.880 --> 00:09:06.320
and two-story homes are about $1,500 to

00:09:03.600 --> 00:09:07.920
$2,500 and up. Every house here will get

00:09:06.320 --> 00:09:10.240
its own quote, but that'll kind of give

00:09:07.920 --> 00:09:12.120
you an idea of the range of dollars that

00:09:10.240 --> 00:09:14.320
you'll be charging. So, say your average

00:09:12.120 --> 00:09:16.280
booking is $1,000, okay? If you were to

00:09:14.320 --> 00:09:18.839
do 10 houses, you're looking at 10

00:09:16.280 --> 00:09:21.640
grand. 20 houses, obviously 20 grand. 40

00:09:18.839 --> 00:09:24.600
or 50 houses, 40 to 50 grand. And 20

00:09:21.640 --> 00:09:27.000
houses over a 5-week install season is

00:09:24.600 --> 00:09:28.560
only about four houses a week. Then

00:09:27.000 --> 00:09:30.760
you've got your maintenance calls and

00:09:28.560 --> 00:09:32.240
your takedowns after the holidays. Now,

00:09:30.760 --> 00:09:33.920
this one does take a little bit more to

00:09:32.240 --> 00:09:36.320
get started than most of the others on

00:09:33.920 --> 00:09:38.840
today's list, so I I probably plan on

00:09:36.320 --> 00:09:40.400
about $2,500 to 4 grand to go ahead and

00:09:38.840 --> 00:09:42.600
get started. And about a thousand

00:09:40.400 --> 00:09:45.280
dollars of that is your first 500 feet

00:09:42.600 --> 00:09:48.400
of C9 lights. One supplier, Christmas

00:09:45.280 --> 00:09:51.840
Designers, sells a 100-foot C9 kit with

00:09:48.400 --> 00:09:53.320
100 bulbs for $199.50. So, five kits

00:09:51.840 --> 00:09:54.720
would run you about a grand. Then, of

00:09:53.320 --> 00:09:57.000
course, you're going to need a ladder, a

00:09:54.720 --> 00:09:58.480
stabilizer, some safety equipment, which

00:09:57.000 --> 00:10:00.560
will probably run you somewhere in the

00:09:58.480 --> 00:10:02.680
neighborhood of 700 to 1,500. And I

00:10:00.560 --> 00:10:04.320
would not recommend that you skimp there

00:10:02.680 --> 00:10:06.040
because you're working on roofs. So,

00:10:04.320 --> 00:10:08.440
this is one of the businesses on the

00:10:06.040 --> 00:10:09.960
list today that, unfortunately, is going

00:10:08.440 --> 00:10:11.320
to be a bit riskier, and there's going

00:10:09.960 --> 00:10:12.680
to be some safety measures that you're

00:10:11.320 --> 00:10:14.160
going to want to take into account. And

00:10:12.680 --> 00:10:15.800
keep in mind, on every new house that

00:10:14.160 --> 00:10:18.000
you take on means that you are going to

00:10:15.800 --> 00:10:19.480
have to buy more lights. Now, the best

00:10:18.000 --> 00:10:21.600
way to start this is to pick a

